¶
{{smaller block|The extent to which ''anthropophagy'' has been carried among some nations is, no doubt, mainly due to the indulgence of the appetite once aroused.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', IV. 808.}}}}

'''anthropophobia''' (an″thrọ̄-pọ̄-fō′bi-ä̤), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἄνθρωπος, man, + -φοβία, < φοβεῖν, fear.] Aversion to man; dread of meeting persons.
{{smaller block|He has ''anthropophobia'', being afraid to meet any one about the house.
{{right|''Alien. and Neurol.'', VI. 144.}}}}

'''anthropophuism''' (an-thrọ̄-pof′ū-izm), ''n.'' [Prop. *''anthropophyism'', < Gr. ἀνθρωποφυής, of man’s nature (< ἄνθρωπος, man, + φυή, nature, < φύειν, produce, in pass. grow), + ''-ism''.] That conception of the gods which attributes to them the possession of functions and desires similar to those of human beings.
{{smaller block|The Jupiter of Homer is to be regarded . . . as the receptacle and butt of the principal parts of such earthly, sensual, and appetitive elements as, at the time of Homer, ''anthropophuism'' had obtruded into the sphere of deity.
{{right|''Gladstone'', Studies in Homer, II. 174.}}}}

'''anthropophuistic''' (an-thrọ̄-pof-ū-is′tik), ''a.'' [As ''anthropophuism'' + ''-ist-ic''.] Relating to or characterized by anthropophuism.
{{smaller block|That introduction of the female principle into the sphere of deity, which the Greeks seem to have adopted, after their ''anthropophuistic'' manner, with a view to the family order among the Immortals.
{{right|''Gladstone'', Studies in Homer, II. 51.}}}}

